--TEST--
`Core\Command::help` names a parameter declared at a class, which is a type no runtime tag can spell and no conversion arm can name — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Parses` needs no `use`: it is one of the interfaces the compiler declares
// for every program. A parameter declared at such a class is the argument a
// command line converts through the class's own `parse`.
class Slug implements Parses
{
    public tainted string $text = "";

    public function constructor(tainted string $text)
    {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    public static function parse(tainted string $s): static
    {
        return new static($s);
    }
}

class Releases
{
    #[Core\Command(name: "publish", about: "Publish one release")]
    public static function publish(
        Slug $target,
        #[Core\Option(about: "The release to publish")] Core\Uuid $release,
    ): uint {
        return 0;
    }
}

// This is the boundary the page's middle column exists for, and the reason it
// is read off the declaration rather than off anything the table already
// carried. Both parameters below reach the matcher as *one* thing each — a
// conversion arm — and a runtime tag is narrower still: a tag says "an object"
// and a conversion says "through some class's `parse`", so neither can say
// which class, and `Slug` and `Core\Uuid` would print the same word. The
// declaration is the only place either name survives.
echo "" . Core\Command::help("publish");
--EXPECT--
usage: publish <target> [--release]

Publish one release

arguments:
  <target>  Slug

options:
  --release  Core\Uuid  The release to publish
